WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.090
- Today's mission is to
intentionally get stuck in traffic.

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:06.030
Check this out, complete standstill.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.283
I've actually really outdone myself here.

00:00:08.283 --> 00:00:10.260
Been here for about five minutes.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:11.093
Fun.

00:00:11.093 --> 00:00:13.200
Inner city traffic can
be a real nightmare.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.030
A great way to spend a hot Thursday.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:17.160
In New York City, the traffic is so bad

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.410
that the MTA estimates New
Yorkers lose an average

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.740
of 117 hours in congestion each year.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.870
But here in London and in Singapore,

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:26.610
we have a thing called congestion tolls

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.290
that aims to reduce traffic

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.320
by charging drivers to enter
busier parts of the city.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.690
And now congestion tolls
are headed to New York.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.760
- It's gonna be implemented
in about a year.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.860
We're actually installing
the infrastructure,

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.140
all the cameras and the sensors right now.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.640
- But here's the thing,

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.350
even though there's a congestion charge

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:46.770
in London and has been for
decades, I'm still stuck

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.380
in traffic and the roads have
actually been getting slower.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.180
So do congestion tolls work?

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:52.590
How will New York use them?

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.140
And are these cameras
headed for other US cities?

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:55.973
Let's find out.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.048
So here's how it works.

00:00:58.048 --> 00:01:00.090
London's congestion charge zone covers

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.040
around eight square miles in the center

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.230
of the city and the boundaries delineated

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.320
by these red circles on
the road and these signs.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.500
I've just crossed over the
congestion charge boundary zone

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.660
where I will be picked up by some cameras

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:16.380
that are just up there that
will register my license plate

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.930
and charge me 15 pounds to drive

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.990
in the zone for the day,
which is around $19.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.000
It's not actually me that'll
be charged, it's my wife

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.440
'cause it's all linked to her account.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:26.610
Sorry, darling.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:29.460
So what's New York's congestion
charge going to look like?

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:30.510
- There you are.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.213
- How you doing? Nice to meet you.

00:01:32.213 --> 00:01:33.046
- How you doing?

00:01:33.046 --> 00:01:33.879
- [George] To answer that,

00:01:33.879 --> 00:01:35.940
here's Metropolitan
Transportation Authority CEO,

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:36.773
Janno Lieber.

00:01:36.773 --> 00:01:39.270
- All of Manhattan, south of 60th Street,

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:43.230
which is just at the
bottom of Central Park,

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:46.170
is going to in effect be a tolled area.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.830
There are two marginal
highways on the west

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:50.970
and east side of the island

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.370
of Manhattan where you
can circumvent the toll.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.110
We think the price is gonna be somewhere

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.810
between 10 and maybe 20 bucks

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.910
and the tolls are gonna
automatically appear

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:01.920
on people's E-ZPasses.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.380
- The MTA told me it was
still finalizing pricing

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.690
but it was considering
discounts and tax credits

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:09.180
for low income frequent
drivers and residents.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.280
It also told me it would
monitor traffic levels

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.170
around the congestion zone to try

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.060
and avoid a buildup
from people avoiding it.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.580
Where will the congestion
charge money be going?

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.070
- It has to be used to improve
the mass transit system

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.500
but we're not just
maintaining what we have.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.140
We're building, extending subway lines.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:26.490
With climate change

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:29.595
and the air quality issues
that all cities are facing,

00:02:29.595 --> 00:02:31.170
there's really no choice.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.510
- The MTA says that 80% of the money

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.550
will be used to improve New York's Subways

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.710
and buses while 10% each will be going

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.860
to the Long Island Railroad
and Metro North Railroad.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:45.030
But implementing a congestion
toll can be pretty divisive.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.520
After all, these tolls
are designed to make one

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.200
of the most convenient forms

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.740
of transport a financial inconvenience.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:54.390
The MTA has already received requests

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:55.890
from over 100 different groups

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.990
that believe they should
be exempt or discounted

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:01.590
from the toll, from
musicians to hearse drivers.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:04.050
- We have even the farmers have claimed

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.490
that they should not be charged.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.980
There are a lot of good stories out there

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:10.320
but the bottom line is,
in the United States,

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.710
we don't have a history of
exempting people from tolls.

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:14.820
- But the loudest critic

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.010
of New York's plan has been New Jersey.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.630
See, folks driving from New Jersey

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.670
into New York already pay between 14

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:24.000
and $17 in tolls when they
use these bridges and tunnels.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.700
New Jersey officials have said
an additional congestion toll

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:29.280
would unfairly burden New Jersey commuters

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.080
and that a fuller
environmental impact study

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.040
should be conducted.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.680
It then sued the Department
of Transportation.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.840
- New Jersey's actually
initiated a lawsuit

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:40.779
against the federal government
for approving our plan.

00:03:40.779 --> 00:03:45.090
And the governor of New York
State has said, respectfully,

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.940
pound sand, this is going to happen.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.460
- So if it's going to happen, can it work?

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.460
Can congestion tolls make roads faster?

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:56.100
Well, New York's plan is
yet to get off the ground

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.970
but London's has been in
place for around two decades.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.490
So I went to Transport for London, or TFL,

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:03.840
the government body responsible

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.520
for London's transport networks,

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.920
kinda like the city's
equivalent of the MTA.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.030
Here's Alina Tuerk.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.500
Are they effective?

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.003
- Yes, they are effective.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.600
We found that when we first
introduced the charge,

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.520
traffic entering the zone dropped

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:21.210
by 18% and congestion dropped by 30%.

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:24.150
- But the problem is London's
roads didn't stay that way.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:28.230
- Over time, congestion levels
have started to go up again.

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:30.840
- TFL data shows that from 2010 to 2019,

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.660
the average speed in
central London got slower

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:37.050
from nine miles an hour to
seven, and delays increased

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:41.010
from around 1.5 minutes per
kilometer to around 2.5.

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:42.630
So what's going on?

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:44.430
Why am I still stuck in traffic?

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:46.950
Well, even though some
drivers can choose to switch

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.260
to a city's public transport network,

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:52.980
others, like for hire vehicles
and delivery drivers, can't.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:54.150
And the number of those drivers

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:55.890
has increased over the years.

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:57.390
Over 50% of the vehicles

00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:59.970
in Manhattan's proposed
congestion tolling area

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:02.160
are for hire vehicles and taxis.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.650
Many cities have also
reallocated road space

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:07.200
to buses, bikes, and pedestrians.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:08.980
- There's now also less
road space available,

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:11.430
hence that balancing out
with the congestion levels.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:12.870
- But even if congestion zones

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.210
don't always make roads quicker,

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:17.070
they can have other benefits.

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:19.200
One is that they can reduce emissions.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.030
TFL say that CO2 emissions

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:24.690
in London have fallen by some
one million tons since 2010

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.760
in part due to the congestion zone.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:30.240
But another is that they can
be very good at raising money,

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.760
whether that was the intended plan or not.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.710
- It's not a money-making scheme

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:37.170
or it hasn't got a
revenue-raising objective,

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:39.693
but nevertheless, it
does make a net surplus.

00:05:39.693 --> 00:05:41.700
(tranquil music)
(door slamming)

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.830
- So with congestion
charging seeming destined

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:45.030
for US streets,

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:46.620
does that mean we're
going to start seeing it

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.600
across other cities in the US?

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.100
Well, Janno hopes so.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:51.690
- I not only think it could work,

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:53.760
I know that other cities are watching

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.110
what we're doing closely.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:57.180
- Cities from LA to Seattle

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:59.130
have been discussing
whether systems like this

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:01.950
could be used to alleviate
their traffic issues.

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:02.970
And in London.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:05.400
- We are getting a lot of interest,

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:07.980
a lot of inquiries from
cities all around the world.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:09.450
- So keep an eye on New York.

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:11.910
While its congestion toll
will likely be the very first

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.201
in America, it might not be the last.

00:06:14.201 --> 00:06:16.868
(lively music)

